# What belongs here: the staging edge. Same routing as the production Caddyfile, with the two
# differences a shared host forces.
#
# 1. TLS COMES FROM A FILE, NOT ACME. A private address is not routable from the internet, so no ACME
#    challenge for it can be answered and Caddy cannot manage this certificate itself. Something
#    outside this stack issues it and renews it; this file only reads it.
#
# 2. THE DASHBOARD AND THE RELAY ARE TOLD APART BY PORT, NOT BY HOSTNAME. Production gives each its
#    own public name. A host with one name has one certificate, so the split moves to the port, and
#    the DSNs the control-plane mints have to name the relay's port. Both blocks otherwise proxy
#    exactly what the production file proxies.
#
# THE HOST IS REQUIRED, AND THIS FILE CANNOT BE THE THING THAT REQUIRES IT. Measured on the image this
# runs against: with the variable absent, every site address below collapses from "name:443" to ":443",
# which Caddy accepts as a valid catch-all rather than rejecting. The edge would then answer to any
# name, and nothing in a deploy would look wrong. So the value is made required where an absence can
# actually stop something, on the edge service in docker-compose.staging.yml, and this file only reads
# what it is given. Do not add a default here: that would hide the absence a second time.
{
	# No ACME account is needed, since every site below serves a certificate from disk. Redirects are
	# off because the published HTTPS port is not 443: an automatic redirect would send a plain-HTTP
	# caller to a port this edge is not reached on, which reads as a broken site rather than as a
	# missing redirect.
	auto_https disable_redirects

	# Per-request metrics, labelled by host. THIS is what records them, and it is the only thing that
	# does. `per_host` adds the host label: without it every series carries only Caddy's internal
	# server name (srv0, srv1), so a request rate cannot say which site it belongs to, and this edge
	# serves the dashboard and the relay from one process.
	#
	# DO NOT PUT A BARE `metrics` INSIDE A SITE BLOCK. It is a terminal handler that serves the
	# Prometheus endpoint, it matches every path when given no matcher, and Caddy orders it ahead of
	# `reverse_proxy`, so the site answers metrics to everything and the proxy is unreachable. The
	# production Caddyfile carries the full account and the measurement; this file must not drift from
	# it, and `deploy/caddy/Caddyfile` is where to read why.
	metrics {
		per_host
	}
}

# The dashboard and the control-plane API on one origin, so the session cookie and the OAuth callbacks
# stay first-party, exactly as in production.
{$CONDUX_STAGING_HOST}:443 {
	tls /etc/condux/tls/staging.crt /etc/condux/tls/staging.key
	encode zstd gzip

	@backend path /api/* /openapi/*
	handle @backend {
		reverse_proxy controlplane:8080
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy web:3000
	}
}

# SDK / OTLP ingest, the stateless relay only. Production reaches this on its own hostname; here it is
# the same name on its own port, which is what the minted DSNs carry.
{$CONDUX_STAGING_HOST}:444 {
	tls /etc/condux/tls/staging.crt /etc/condux/tls/staging.key
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy relay:9000
}

# Prometheus metrics. NOT published to any host port: it is reachable only on the compose network, by
# the monitoring stack. The `metrics` directive serves the Prometheus endpoint at /metrics.
:2020 {
	metrics
}
